@brief
    FLAG-62  `expressCheckout` has been at 100% for six weeks. Retire
             it. The flag goes, the branch it guarded stays, and the
             old path goes with the flag.

Four files:

  1. checkout.provider.ts — `checkout` branches on the flag. The
     express path is now the only path: it keeps its code but loses a
     level of indentation, and the old charge-then-update path below it
     goes entirely.

  2. flags.json — the entry goes.

  3. flags.ts — the field goes.

  4. checkout.resolver.ts — the flag is no longer reported to the
     client, so that line goes too.

CHANGELOG.md records the rollout and stays as it is.

The interesting file is the provider. Deleting a conditional is not
deleting lines that contain the condition — it is promoting one branch
and deleting the other, and the promotion is an indentation change.

@keys
| `dd` | delete this line |
| `6<<` | shift six lines one level left |
| `8dd` | delete eight lines |
| `:g/pat/d` | delete every matching line in this buffer |
| `:bn` `:wqa` | next buffer, write everything |

@hints
Do the provider in three moves, in this order: remove the `if` line,
dedent the branch that survives, then remove everything from the `if`'s
closing brace down to the end of the old path.
--
`<<` shifts a line left by one `shiftwidth`, and it takes a count like
everything else. The surviving branch is six lines, so `6<<` from its
first line does the whole promotion in three keystrokes.
--
After the dedent the cursor is still on the first line of the branch.
`6j` lands on the `}` that used to close the `if`, and from there `8dd`
takes that brace, the blank line, the old charge call and the old update
block.

@solution
`/if (this.flags.expressCheckout)` `Enter` `dd` `6<<` `6j` `8dd`, then
`:bn` `:g/expressCheckout/d` three times, then `:wqa`. 123 keystrokes.

**`<<` with a count is the promotion.** The usual instinct after deleting
an `if` is to visually select the block and press `<`, which works and
costs a mode change and a motion. `6<<` is the same thing in three
keystrokes and leaves the cursor where it started, which is what makes
the `6j` afterwards countable. If you would rather not count, `>i{` and
`<i{` shift everything inside the surrounding braces — but here the
braces you want are the ones you are about to delete, so a count is
simpler.

**Delete the branch, not the condition.** The line that mentions
`expressCheckout` is one of fifteen lines this change removes from the
provider. Searching for the flag name and deleting the lines that match
it — which is what `:g/expressCheckout/d` would do here — leaves both
branches in place, back to back, and the second one unreachable. That is
a real review comment on real flag-removal MRs, and it is why this file
is the one part of the ticket you do by hand.

**The other three are `:g`.** In the JSON, the type and the resolver, the
flag genuinely is one line each, identified by its name, and the same
command works in all three buffers. Two shapes of change, two tools, in
one ticket.

One habit worth keeping for flag removal specifically: work the flag out
of the code first and delete the flag's definition last. If you delete
the definition first, the compiler stops being able to tell you where the
remaining uses are, and you are back to grepping.

@start src/domain/order/checkout.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class CheckoutProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });
  }

  public async checkout(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (this.flags.expressCheckout) {
      await this.paymentService.authorise(order.id, order.total);

      return this.orderRepository.update({
        where: { id },
        values: { status: 'PENDING' },
      });
    }

    await this.paymentService.charge(order.id, order.total);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'PENDING' },
    });
  }
}

@expected src/domain/order/checkout.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class CheckoutProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });
  }

  public async checkout(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    await this.paymentService.authorise(order.id, order.total);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'PENDING' },
    });
  }
}

